1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:12,890
Meet the Big Tiny Operations Brain. This proof of concept explores a simple
idea: spend less time organising updates, and make the next action easier to
see.

2
00:00:14,050 --> 00:00:36,970
For a property operations team, the challenge is often turning scattered
updates into a reliable handover. Which issue is still open? Who owns the
next step? Has a follow up changed the plan? Our hypothesis is that
preparing and checking this information is a useful place to reduce
administrative effort.

3
00:00:38,119 --> 00:00:58,139
The proposed value is straightforward: clearer handovers, fewer duplicate
tasks, and less time chasing context. The current proof of concept
demonstrates the workflow. It does not yet prove a productivity gain. That
is what a focused pilot should establish.

4
00:00:59,291 --> 00:01:19,811
Here is how it works. Streamlit provides the workspace. LangGraph
coordinates three specialist roles. They use the same on premises G X ten
model through Tailscale. SQLite remembers approved tasks, so each new update
has context.

5
00:01:22,104 --> 00:01:35,974
This is the working application. We will use fictional maintenance notes and
real model responses. Notes are stored when submitted, but task changes
always wait for a person to approve.

6
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:48,650
First, a staff member reports a dripping tap at Cedar Grove cabin. Maya
needs to arrange a plumber. The note also includes a deadline.

7
00:01:49,402 --> 00:02:01,572
The extractor finds the facts. The coordinator checks saved tasks and
proposes what should happen next. The reviewer checks that the proposal is
supported by the note.

8
00:02:02,305 --> 00:02:16,515
Here is the proposed task, with an owner, a due date, and the original
evidence. The reviewer has passed it, but the final decision is still yours.
Let us approve it.

9
00:02:18,190 --> 00:02:26,830
The first task is now saved in local memory. This is what allows the brain
to connect future updates to the same issue.

10
00:02:27,585 --> 00:02:39,125
Now there is a follow up. Maya has booked the plumber, and the visit is
scheduled for November twenty fourth. The repair is not yet complete.

11
00:02:39,921 --> 00:02:50,861
This time, the coordinator receives the existing task as context. It can
propose an update to the same record instead of creating another task.

12
00:02:51,604 --> 00:03:03,174
The proposed change keeps the same task identity and revises the visit date.
After approval, there is still one task, and it remains in progress.

13
00:03:07,383 --> 00:03:18,203
Finally, someone asks to mark the maintenance job complete, but cannot say
which cabin or which job. This is where guessing would be a problem.

14
00:03:18,942 --> 00:03:29,982
The workflow checks the request against what it knows. An unclear completion
request should become a question, not an unsupported change to a property
record.

15
00:03:30,719 --> 00:03:41,399
The result is a clarification. We can acknowledge the question and provide
more detail later. The existing task stays unfinished.

16
00:03:45,623 --> 00:04:08,713
Today, the system can propose new tasks, update remembered tasks, and ask
for clarification. It does not connect to booking systems, contact vendors,
or make purchases. Property readiness checks, procedure lookup, and weekly
reporting are possible next steps, if staff evidence supports them.

17
00:04:09,855 --> 00:04:33,135
Our recommendation is a small pilot for daily handovers and maintenance
follow ups. Name an operations owner, select a representative property
group, and evaluate approved examples with staff. Keep the current
operational register authoritative. Compare this workflow with the manual
process and a simpler single prompt.

18
00:04:34,287 --> 00:04:59,297
Measure net staff time, including checking and corrections. Also measure
missed actions, wrong task matches, and unsupported changes. A proposed
initial target is a twenty five percent reduction in median active staff
time, with no critical incorrect changes accepted. These are pilot targets,
not achieved results.

19
00:05:00,437 --> 00:05:25,277
The decision requested is approval to run a bounded discovery and pilot,
with an owner, authorised examples, and agreed success measures. If the
evidence is strong, extend one workflow at a time. If it is not, revise or
stop. Start small, keep people in control, and let measured results guide
the investment.
